## Intake requirements

Collect only the minimum information necessary for the stated process. Route restricted material to the approved private system rather than embedding it in public documentation.

| Intake item | Why it is needed | Public or restricted | Accepted format | Required |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Request type and concise summary | Routing | Public-safe summary | Replace | Yes |
| Asset, product, order, or incident reference | Scope resolution | Usually restricted | Replace | Replace |
| Requestor identity and authority | Standing/authorization review | Restricted | Approved private channel | Replace |
| Governing agreement or policy reference | Authority review | Reference may be public; body may be restricted | Replace | Replace |
| Supporting evidence | Bounded verification | Restricted unless approved | Approved private channel | Replace |
| Requested outcome | Triage only; not entitlement proof | Public-safe summary | Replace | Yes |

## Review process

<Steps>
  <Step title="Acknowledge and classify">
    Record a bounded receipt, classify the issue, and identify the controlling source. Acknowledgment is not acceptance, approval, or entitlement.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Check authority and completeness">
    Verify reviewer authority, requestor standing where applicable, minimum evidence, privacy boundary, conflicts, holds, and qualified-review triggers.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Review the exact scope">
    Compare the request with the current governing instrument and documented evidence. Preserve unresolved contradictions.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Issue a bounded outcome">
    State what was decided, by whom, under which authority, for what scope and period, and what remains unresolved.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Record and escalate">
    Preserve the public-safe status, restricted evidence reference, correction path, and authorized escalation. Do not expose the evidence body.
  </Step>
</Steps>

